# Counter Management

{info:title=Global Sequential Numbering System}
The Counter Management System provides unique sequential numbering across all blockchain tables in the PostgreSQL instance, ensuring proper ordering and enabling efficient hash chain verification through a sophisticated shared memory architecture with lazy recovery mechanisms.
{info}

{toc:printable=true|style=disc|maxLevel=3}

## Overview

### Why Global Counters?

{panel:title=Global Counter Rationale|borderColor=#2196F3|titleBGColor=#E3F2FD}
Traditional PostgreSQL approaches like LSN (Log Sequence Number) have limitations for blockchain use:

**LSN Limitations:**
* Not guaranteed to be unique per row
* Can change during WAL replay
* Not suitable for cryptographic inclusion
* Recovery scenarios can cause duplicates

**Global Counter Benefits:**
* Unique across all blockchain tables
* Persistent across restarts
* Included in hash computation
* Enables cross-table ordering
{panel}

### Counter Architecture

{code:title=Counter System Architecture}
Application Layer
    ↓
Blockchain TAM
    ↓
Counter Management System
├── Shared Memory Counters ← Active counter state
├── File Persistence ← Crash recovery
├── Lazy Recovery ← Performance optimization
└── Table Registration ← Per-table management
    ↓
PostgreSQL Shared Memory
    ↓
File System (counter.dat)
{code}

## Implementation Architecture

### Shared Memory Structure

{expand:title=Counter Shared Memory Implementation}
{code:c|title=Counter Data Structures}
// Main counter management structure
typedef struct BlockchainCounterData
{
    LWLock *lock;                    // Synchronization lock
    uint64 global_counter;           // Current global counter value
    int num_tables;                  // Number of registered tables
    time_t last_persist_time;        // Last persistence timestamp
    bool recovery_needed;            // Lazy recovery flag
    BlockchainTableCounter tables[MAX_BLOCKCHAIN_TABLES];
} BlockchainCounterData;

// Per-table counter information
typedef struct BlockchainTableCounter
{
    Oid table_oid;                   // Table identifier
    uint64 last_counter;             // Last assigned counter for this table
    uint64 max_counter_in_table;     // Highest counter found in table data
    bool needs_recovery;             // Table-specific recovery flag
    char table_name[NAMEDATALEN];    // Table name for debugging
} BlockchainTableCounter;

// Shared memory segment
static BlockchainCounterData *counter_data = NULL;
{code}
{expand}

### Memory Layout and Access

{section}
{column:width=50%}
{panel:title=Shared Memory Layout|borderColor=#4CAF50|titleBGColor=#E8F5E8}
**Fixed Allocation:**
* Base structure: 4KB
* Per-table entries: 128 bytes each
* Maximum tables: 1000 (configurable)
* Total maximum: ~132KB

**Access Patterns:**
* Read-heavy for counter queries
* Write during INSERT operations
* Batch updates during recovery
{panel}
{column}

{column:width=50%}
{panel:title=Synchronization Strategy|borderColor=#FF9800|titleBGColor=#FFF3E0}
**LWLock Usage:**
* Shared locks for counter reads
* Exclusive locks for increments
* Batch operations minimize lock time

**Lock Granularity:**
* Single global lock for simplicity
* Per-table locks consideration for future
* Lock-free reads for queries
{panel}
{column}
{section}

### Counter Allocation Algorithm

{expand:title=Counter Increment Implementation}
{code:c|title=BlockchainGetNextCounter Function}
uint64
BlockchainGetNextCounter(Oid table_oid)
{
    uint64 next_counter;
    int table_index;
    
    // Ensure shared memory is initialized
    if (counter_data == NULL)
        ereport(ERROR, (errmsg("Counter system not initialized")));
    
    // Acquire exclusive lock for modification
    LWLockAcquire(&counter_data->lock, LW_EXCLUSIVE);
    
    // Find or create table entry
    table_index = find_or_create_table_entry(table_oid);
    if (table_index < 0)
    {
        LWLockRelease(&counter_data->lock);
        ereport(ERROR, (errmsg("Too many blockchain tables")));
    }
    
    // Perform lazy recovery if needed
    if (counter_data->tables[table_index].needs_recovery)
    {
        perform_lazy_recovery(table_oid, table_index);
        counter_data->tables[table_index].needs_recovery = false;
    }
    
    // Increment global counter
    counter_data->global_counter++;
    next_counter = counter_data->global_counter;
    
    // Update table-specific counter
    counter_data->tables[table_index].last_counter = next_counter;
    
    // Schedule persistence if needed
    schedule_persistence_if_needed();
    
    LWLockRelease(&counter_data->lock);
    
    return next_counter;
}
{code}
{expand}

## Persistence and Recovery

### File-Based Persistence

{panel:title=Counter Persistence Strategy|borderColor=#673AB7|titleBGColor=#EDE7F6}
Counter state is persisted to disk for crash recovery:

**Persistence File Format:**
* Location: `$PGDATA/blockchain_counter.dat`
* Binary format for efficiency
* Atomic write with temporary file
* Checksum for corruption detection

**Persistence Triggers:**
* Periodic background process (configurable interval)
* Manual flush during shutdown
* After significant counter increments
* Before backup operations
{panel}

### Lazy Recovery Mechanism

{expand:title=Lazy Recovery Implementation}
{code:c|title=Lazy Recovery Algorithm}
static void
perform_lazy_recovery(Oid table_oid, int table_index)
{
    Relation rel;
    ScanKeyData scankey;
    SysScanDesc scan;
    HeapTuple tuple;
    uint64 max_counter_found = 0;
    bool found_any = false;
    
    // Open the blockchain table
    rel = relation_open(table_oid, AccessShareLock);
    
    // Scan table to find highest counter value
    scan = systable_beginscan(rel, InvalidOid, false, NULL, 0, NULL);
    
    while ((tuple = systable_getnext(scan)) != NULL)
    {
        uint64 row_counter = extract_counter_from_tuple(tuple);
        if (row_counter > max_counter_found)
        {
            max_counter_found = row_counter;
            found_any = true;
        }
    }
    
    systable_endscan(scan);
    relation_close(rel, AccessShareLock);
    
    // Update shared memory with recovered counter
    if (found_any)
    {
        counter_data->tables[table_index].max_counter_in_table = max_counter_found;
        
        // Update global counter if this table has higher values
        if (max_counter_found > counter_data->global_counter)
        {
            counter_data->global_counter = max_counter_found;
            elog(LOG, "Global counter recovered from table %u: %lu", 
                 table_oid, max_counter_found);
        }
    }
    
    counter_data->tables[table_index].needs_recovery = false;
}
{code}

**Lazy Recovery Benefits:**
* Eliminates startup delays
* Recovery only when table is accessed
* Handles partial recovery scenarios
* Maintains performance for active tables
{expand}

### Recovery Scenarios

{section}
{column:width=50%}
{panel:title=Normal Recovery|borderColor=#4CAF50|titleBGColor=#E8F5E8}
**Clean Shutdown:**
* Counter file exists and is valid
* Global counter restored from file
* Per-table counters verified against data

**Process:**
1. Read counter file during startup
2. Validate checksums
3. Mark tables for lazy verification
4. Resume counter allocation
{panel}
{column}

{column:width=50%}
{panel:title=Crash Recovery|borderColor=#FF5722|titleBGColor=#FBE9E7}
**Unclean Shutdown:**
* Counter file may be stale
* Full table scans needed for accuracy
* Global counter reconstructed

**Process:**
1. Mark all tables for lazy recovery
2. Scan tables as they're accessed
3. Reconstruct global counter
4. Persist corrected state
{panel}
{column}
{section}

## Performance Characteristics

### Counter Operation Performance

{section}
{column:width=50%}
| Operation | Time | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Counter Increment** | <1µs | Shared memory operation |
| **Table Registration** | ~10µs | First access to new table |
| **Lazy Recovery** | 1-1000ms | Depends on table size |
| **Persistence** | ~1ms | Background operation |
{column}

{column:width=50%}
{panel:title=Scalability Characteristics|borderColor=#FF9800|titleBGColor=#FFF3E0}
**Memory Usage:**
* O(1) base memory
* O(n) with number of blockchain tables
* Configurable maximum tables

**Lock Contention:**
* Minimal for read operations
* Brief exclusive locks for increments
* Lock-free counter queries
{panel}
{column}
{section}

### Throughput Analysis

{expand:title=Counter System Throughput}
**Single-Threaded Performance:**
* ~1,000,000 counter increments per second
* Limited by memory bandwidth and lock overhead
* Consistent performance regardless of counter value

**Multi-Threaded Performance:**
* Lock contention becomes limiting factor
* ~100,000 increments/second with 8 threads
* Performance scales with reduced lock duration

**Optimization Strategies:**
* Batch counter allocation for bulk operations
* Lock-free reads for counter queries
* Per-table locks for future enhancement
{expand}

## System Integration

### Integration with Blockchain TAM

{panel:title=TAM-Counter Coordination|borderColor=#9C27B0|titleBGColor=#F3E5F5}
The counter system is tightly integrated with the Blockchain Table Access Method:

**Insert Operation Flow:**
1. TAM requests next counter
2. Counter system provides unique value
3. TAM includes counter in hash computation
4. TAM stores row with counter value
5. Counter system may persist state

**Atomic Guarantees:**
* Counter allocation and row insertion are atomic
* No possibility of counter reuse
* Transaction rollback handles failed inserts
{panel}

### PostgreSQL Process Integration

{expand:title=Process Lifecycle Integration}
{code:c|title=Process Integration Points}
// Extension initialization
void
_PG_init(void)
{
    // Initialize counter system
    BlockchainCounterShmemInit();
    
    // Register shared memory requirements
    RequestAddinShmemSpace(BlockchainCounterShmemSize());
    RequestNamedLWLockTranche("blockchain_counter", 1);
    
    // Register hooks
    prev_shmem_startup_hook = shmem_startup_hook;
    shmem_startup_hook = blockchain_counter_shmem_startup;
}

// Shared memory initialization
static void
blockchain_counter_shmem_startup(void)
{
    bool found;
    
    // Chain to previous hook
    if (prev_shmem_startup_hook)
        prev_shmem_startup_hook();
    
    // Initialize or attach to shared memory
    counter_data = ShmemInitStruct("BlockchainCounterData",
                                   BlockchainCounterShmemSize(),
                                   &found);
    
    if (!found)
    {
        // First time initialization
        initialize_counter_shared_memory();
        load_counter_state_from_file();
    }
}
{code}
{expand}

## SQL Interface and Functions

### Counter Query Functions

{expand:title=Counter Management SQL Functions}
{code:sql|title=Counter SQL Interface}
-- Get current global counter value
SELECT get_current_global_counter();

-- Get next counter value for a table (preview only, doesn't increment)
SELECT get_next_counter_preview('audit_log');

-- Get counter statistics for all blockchain tables
SELECT * FROM get_blockchain_counters();

-- Force counter persistence to disk
SELECT persist_blockchain_counters();

-- Trigger recovery for a specific table
SELECT recover_table_counters('audit_log');

-- Get counter range for a table
SELECT 
    table_name,
    min_counter,
    max_counter,
    counter_count
FROM get_table_counter_range('audit_log');
{code}
{expand}

